{{ artifact_naming(chain=false) }}

All artifacts live in the ADR sidecar, never in a repository of the scope. Under the ADR
root:

- the **project dir**, `<ADR root>/YYYY/MM/DD-<project slug>/`, made at
  setup: `project.md`; `driver-record.md`; `plan-c<C>-g<G>.md`;
  `notes-plan-user-c<C>-g<G>.md`; `exploration-c<C>-g<G>-<N>.md`;
  `state-c<C>.md`; `exception-spike-c<C>.md`{% if not gate %}; the plan review's
  `notes-plan-review-c<C>-g<G>.md`, `dispositions-plan-c<C>-g<G>-a<A>.md`
  and `judge-verdict-plan-c<C>-g<G>-a<A>.md` — here, not in the cycle dir,
  which a build's orchestrator owns{% endif %}; and one **publish dir** per
  repository, `publish/<basename>/`, holding that repository's publish
  records alone — `c<C>.md`, or `c<C>-<n>.md` for the `n`th publish of the
  same cycle, `now-<n>.md` for a "publish now", and the exception paths
  `exception-c<C>.md`, `exception-c<C>-<n>.md` and `exception-now-<n>.md`.
  A repository's **publish records** are the files in its publish dir not
  named `exception-*`, plus, for the primary alone, the `publish-*.md`
  files directly in the project dir, which a driver without publish dirs
  wrote;
- one **cycle dir** per cycle, `<ADR root>/YYYY/MM/DD-<project
  slug>--<cycle slug>/`, made by the forester when it writes the cycle's
  `request.md`. A build cycle's dir is the orchestrator's working dir and
  everything in it is the orchestrator's; a spike's holds the
  experimenter's report and log.

Ordinals: **cycle `C`** from 1, task-wide, never reused; **plan attempt
`G`** from 1 within a cycle — a rejected, replanned or escalated plan bumps
it{% if gate %}.{% else %}; **review round `A`**, 1 or 2, within a plan attempt — the
judge's one rework is round 2. Every plan review path carries `c<C>-g<G>`,
and the dispositions and verdict paths carry `a<A>` too, so both are
recoverable from a path on resume.{% endif +%}

## Driver record

`<project dir>/driver-record.md`, append-only: one line per event, appended
as it happens with `printf '%s\n' '<line>' >> <project dir>/driver-record.md`,
never edited. Header, written at setup:

```
project: <slug>
repo: <primary's abs path>
scope: <abs path>,<abs path>,…
adr root: <abs path>
base branch: <basename>=<name>;…
start head: <basename>=<sha>;…
branch prefix: <prefix>
remote: <name>
publish: off | push | prs
```

Then, per cycle:

```
cycle <C> plan: <plan path>
cycle <C> plan escalated: <plan path> session <ccc session id>
cycle <C> plan done: <plan path>
{% if not gate %}
cycle <C> plan written: build|spike|done <plan path> request <request path|none>
cycle <C> plan review escalated: review <notes path> session <ccc session id>
cycle <C> plan review escalated: respond <dispositions path> session <ccc session id>
cycle <C> plan review escalated: judge <verdict path>
{% endif %}
cycle <C> planned: build|spike <plan path> request <request path>
cycle <C> started: build|spike <cycle dir> head <list> branch <list>
cycle <C> session: <ccc session id>
cycle <C> ended: shipped head <list> branch <list> stack <list>
cycle <C> ended: written head <list> branch <list> branches <list>
cycle <C> ended: stopped head <list> branch <list> leftover <list>
cycle <C> digested: <state path>
cycle <C> publish: <basename>=<publish path>
cycle <C> published: <basename>=<publish path>
cycle <C> publish skipped: <basename>=<publish path>
```

and, between cycles or during a build, when the scope changes:

```
scope added: <abs path> head <sha> branch <name>
scope dropped: <abs path>
```

each followed by a new `scope:` line, which supersedes the one before.

**Qualified lists.** The `scope:` line lists every repository, primary
first. Every per-repository value — `base branch` and `start head` in the
header, and `head`, `branch`, `stack`, `branches` and `leftover` on cycle
lines — is a qualified list: `<basename>=<value>` entries separated by
`;`, in scope order, one per repository; a value that is itself a list
keeps its `,` inside its entry, and an empty one is `none`. A value with no
`=` is the primary's: that is how a record written before the scope existed
reads, and a record with no `scope:` line is a scope of one.

{% if not gate %}
A `plan written` line follows every plan-mode or respond-mode launch of a
plan attempt that replies `OK`; the attempt's last one holds its kind and
request. A `plan review escalated` line records a stop inside the plan
review; its step word says which launch stopped and so which stop a resume
re-enters. The `review` form names the reviewer's notes target, a reviewer
that wrote no status line. The `respond` form names the response's dispositions target,
whose `a<A>` is the round the resumed reply goes to the judge at; the
`judge` form carries no session: a judge escalation is arbitrated by the
user in words, never answered by resuming the judge. `plan escalated`
keeps its one meaning: a plan-mode launch stopped. `planned` is the line
step 3 reads.

{% endif %}
An empty list is written `none`. Your queries on the record are three: the
last line for cycle `C`; the header fields, the last `scope:` line
included; and the lists on the `ended` lines. A repository's **start HEAD**
and **base branch** are the header's entries for it, or its `scope added`
line's `head` and `branch` when it joined later.

- A repository's **project stack** is its entry on every `ended: shipped`
  line's `stack`, concatenated in cycle order, bottom first.
- A repository's **recorded refs** are every ref on its entries of any
  `stack`, `branches` or `leftover` list of any cycle. A ref one cycle
  recorded is never a later cycle's finding.

A `publish` directive at a stop that changes the setting for the session
("publish: prs from now on") appends a new `publish: <setting>` line; the
last one stands.

## State you track

| State | Initial | Changes when |
|---|---|---|
| **primary**, **ADR root**, **project dir**, **project slug** | setup | never |
| **scope** | setup, or the record's last `scope:` line on resume | a scope phrase or a `drop from scope:` at a driver stop (**Scope**) |
| **base branch** °, **start HEAD** ° | setup, the record on resume, or the repository's `scope added` line | never |
| **branch prefix**, **remote** | setup, or the record's header on resume | never |
| **publish** | setup, or the record's last `publish:` header line | a user directive |
| **limits** | `--count N` (cycles digested this session) and `--until HH:MM`, as the user gave them | never |
| **start time** | the clock at setup | never |
| **digested** | 0 | each digest that replies `written` |
| **C**, **G** | 0 and unset, or from the record on resume | Loop step 2 |
{% if not gate %}
| **A** | unset, or from the record on resume | Loop step 2c |
{% endif %}
| **current cycle** | none, or the open cycle on resume | its kind, dir, plan path and request path at `planned`; pre-cycle HEAD and branch ° at `started`, or a repository's `scope added` line when it joins during the cycle; session id at `session` |
| **latest state** | the last `digested` line's path, or `none` | each digest |
| **HEAD** ° | `git rev-parse HEAD` in the repository | after every launch |

Rows marked ° are kept per repository of the scope. Nothing else. Paths and lists are taken from the record or built from the
naming above; options are transcribed from the user, never interpreted.

## Standard payload

Every subagent runs with `--cwd <primary>`, the publisher excepted (Loop
step 5). Every prompt carries the absolute paths it needs, the inputs its
step lists, and one instruction: "Write to file. Reply with the status line
only." Where a step lists **the scope**, the prompt carries every
repository's path in scope order, primary first, and where the step lists a
per-repository value, that value beside each path. The orchestrator is the
exception: its prompt is its request path, the prefix words and, when the
scope holds more than the primary, a scope phrase naming every other
repository — `"<cycle dir>/request.md branch prefix: <prefix> scope:
<path2> <path3>"` — nothing else. The primary is never in the phrase.

Each launch below lists only additions (+).

## Replies

Every subagent ends its reply with one line:

```
[agent] status=<OK|RETRY|ESCALATE> result=<token|-> out=<abs-path|-> commit=<hash|->
```

`OK` completed; `result` says which normal outcome. `ESCALATE` goes to the
user: `out` is the document saying why. You never read `out`; you pass it on.
**A missing or unparsable line is `ESCALATE`** with `out` = the target path
you gave the agent, or, from the orchestrator, `-`.

| Role | `OK` result |
|---|---|
| project-forester, plan | `build` · `spike` · `done` |
{% if not gate %}
| project-forester, respond | `build` · `spike` · `done` |
{% endif %}
| project-forester, digest | `written` |
{% if not gate %}
| plan-reviewer | `findings` · `no-findings` |
| judge | `approved` · `rework` |
{% endif %}
| orchestrator | `shipped` · `gate` |
| experimenter | `written`, + `commit` |
| publisher | `published` · `pushed` · `unpushed` |

## Flow

### Setup

1. The user's words name the **primary** (the `--cwd` of every launch),
   the ADR root, and either an existing project dir — a resume — or a new
   project: a brief, a path to a spec document, or both, and a **project
   slug**. Options, each transcribed and never interpreted: `branch prefix:
   <p>` (default: the project slug), `remote: <name>` (default `origin`),
   `publish: off|push|prs` (default `off`), a scope phrase (**Scope**),
   `--count N`, `--until HH:MM`.
   A new project with no slug → ask for one once, end turn. Anything else,
   or anything you cannot transcribe as one of those → ask the user to
   restate it, end turn.
2. **New project.** The scope's **Repository checks** (**Scope**) in every
   repository of the scope, the primary first, then:
   - The slug: `git check-ref-format --branch "<slug>"` succeeds, the slug
     holds no `--`, and no path matches `<ADR root>/*/*/??-<slug>` or
     `<ADR root>/*/*/??-<slug>--*` — no project or cycle dir in
     the ADR root carries the slug yet, so every dir named for it is this
     project's → else STOP, quote the command or the matches, ask for
     another slug, end turn.

   Create the project dir `<ADR root>/YYYY/MM/DD-<slug>/`, today's date.
   Write
   `project.md`: first this line,

   > *Provenance: the user's own words, transcribed from chat by the project orchestrator.*

   then the user's brief verbatim, then `Spec: <abs path>` for a named
   spec. Write the record's header, its `scope:` line listing the scope,
   and its `base branch` and `start head` qualified lists. `C` = 0. Loop
   step 1.
3. **Resume.** Read `driver-record.md` in the named project dir; take the
   header's fields; the last `scope:` line is the scope, none → the primary
   alone; `scope added` and `scope dropped` lines are not re-applied, and a
   `scope added` line is read only for its repository's head and branch.
   `C` = the highest cycle number in it, or 0. The last
   line for cycle `C` decides where the loop re-enters. When it is `plan`,
   `plan escalated`, `plan done`{% if not gate %}, `plan written`, `plan
   review escalated`{% endif %} or `planned`, `G` = the `g` of the{% if gate %} plan{% else %} first{% endif +%}
   path on it; otherwise `G` is unused until Loop step 2a sets it.
   - `started` or `session` with no `ended` after it → an open cycle: STOP,
     name the cycle dir and the session id from its `session` line, or,
     with none, say the id is in ccc's audit log; end turn. `resume:
     <words>` → `ccc resume <id> "<words>"`, routed by Loop step 3 as the
     cycle's kind says. `close` → Loop step 3's `skip` route for that
     kind, its HEAD requirement included, then Loop step 4.

   Every other state first passes the **HEAD check (record)**. Then:
   - `plan` → that plan launch was cut off with the session: `G` += 1,
     Loop step 2b; the interrupted plan's path, if the file exists, is one
     of the earlier plan paths, and its target path is never reused.
   - `plan escalated` → the plan's question is still open: Loop step 2b's
     `ESCALATE` STOP again, with the plan path and the recorded session id.
{% if not gate %}
   - `plan written` → the plan review was cut off with the session: `G` +=
     1, Loop step 2b — as for `plan`; the interrupted attempt's documents
     stay as record, and its target paths are never reused.
   - `plan review escalated` → the stop is still open, by its step word.
     `review` → Loop step 2c item 1's STOP again, with the recorded notes
     path and session id. `A` = the `a` of the path on a `respond` or
     `judge` line. `respond` → Loop step 2c item 2's
     `ESCALATE` STOP again, with the recorded dispositions path and session
     id; the resumed reply is routed by that item at round `A`, so it goes
     to the judge at round `A` — `judge-verdict-plan-c<C>-g<G>-a1.md` for
     round 1, `…-a2.md` told "round 2 — approved or escalate only" for
     round 2 — never to the reviewer and never to a target that exists.
     `judge` → Loop step 2c item 3's `ESCALATE` STOP again, with the
     verdict path.
{% endif %}
   - `plan done` → **End**, with the plan path — unless the user's words
     ask for another plan: then Loop step 1, and the next cycle is `C` + 1.
   - `planned` → with the recorded plan and request paths,
{%- if gate %} the **Cycle gate** (Loop step 2b).
{%- else %} Loop step 3.
{%- endif %}

   - `ended` → Loop step 4.
   - `digested` → Loop step 5 when the cycle ended `shipped` and publish
     is not `off`; otherwise Loop step 1.
   - `publish` → Loop step 5 at the repository the line names: that
     publish launch ended with no outcome recorded.
   - `published` or `publish skipped` → Loop step 5 at the next repository
     in scope order whose project stack is not `none`, or Loop step 1 when
     none follows the one the line names.
   - No cycle yet → Loop step 1.

   One driver per project at a time: a second session on the same record
   would interleave it.
4. **Project commit**.

### Scope

The **scope** is the ordered list of code repositories the project may
change: the primary first, then each repository a scope phrase added, in
the order given. Only the user's words widen it — a **scope phrase**, `in
scope:`, `also in scope:` or `scope:` followed by one or more paths, at
setup or at a stop as below. Nothing else does: not `project.md`, not a
plan, not a request, not the state.

**Repository checks**, per repository, at setup and for every path a scope
phrase adds; every command runs as `git -C <path>`. Any failing → STOP,
name the path and the check, quote the command and its output, end turn:

- `rev-parse --show-toplevel` succeeds, and that toplevel is what is
  recorded, so a subdirectory names its repository. An added path whose
  toplevel is the primary or a repository already in scope is refused by
  name. `git -C <ADR root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` succeeds and differs
  from every repository's toplevel: artifacts live in a sidecar repository,
  never in a repository the stacks are built in.
- No two repositories of the scope share a basename → STOP naming both:
  basenames qualify every record value and every file reference.
- `symbolic-ref --short HEAD` → its base branch; it fails (detached HEAD)
  → STOP, ask the user which branch to work on, end turn. `rev-parse HEAD`
  → its start HEAD. `rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge`, `rebase-apply`:
  either exists → STOP, a rebase or `git am` is in progress, end turn.
- The prefix: `git check-ref-format --branch "<prefix>/x"` succeeds;
  for every leading path of the prefix — `p1`, `p1/p2`, … through
  `<prefix>` itself — `git show-ref --verify --quiet refs/heads/<that
  path>` fails (a branch named any of them blocks every `<prefix>/…`
  branch); and `git for-each-ref refs/heads/<prefix>/` prints nothing
  (the prefix is not in use) → else the user picks another prefix or clears
  the refs; a ref a kept leftover left there is named with the rest.
- The record's delimiters: the toplevel contains no `,`, and an added
  one no whitespace — the `scope:` line and the orchestrator's scope
  phrase separate paths with them; its basename contains no `=` or `;`;
  the base branch and the prefix contain no `;` or `,` — qualified lists
  separate entries with `;` and list items with `,`. One does → STOP
  naming the value: the user renames it.

**At every stop** you surface, when the scope holds more than the primary,
one line states it back: `scope: <path> (<branch>), <path> (<branch>)`,
each repository's current branch, so the user sees what the next cycle may
write to before answering.

**Changes after setup** are **User directives**, by where they arrive:

- *Between cycles* — any stop of yours with no cycle open: the cycle gate,
  a plan escalation, a plan-review stop, a digest or publish stop, **End**.
  A scope phrase runs the **Repository checks** for each new path, all of
  them before any is recorded; then for each, in order, append `scope
  added: <toplevel> head <sha> branch <name>`, then one new `scope:` line.
  `drop from scope: <path>` appends `scope dropped: <toplevel>` and a new
  `scope:` line. The primary is refused by name. So is a repository whose
  `stack` or `branches` entries on any `ended` line name a ref — its
  project stack or its spike branches — the refusal naming those refs:
  they are the project's output, and publish, digest and **End** run over
  the scope, so a repository that left it would drop its stack from every
  later publish and from the closing summary. Its `leftover` entries do
  not refuse the drop: a stopped cycle's leftovers are the user's to delete,
  named at the stop that recorded them. Then surface the stop again; the
  user's next words answer it.
- *During a build* — an orchestrator `gate` or `ESCALATE` stop, or
  **Resume**'s stop on an open build. A scope
  phrase is both yours and the orchestrator's. Run the **Repository
  checks** for each new path, plus Loop step 3's check that the cycle
  branch's name is free, run in that path, since the orchestrator creates
  that branch there as the path joins. A check fails → STOP in your own words, naming the path and the
  check, end turn; nothing is relayed and the user restates. All pass →
  append `scope added:` and `scope:` as between cycles, *then* relay the
  whole utterance verbatim to the orchestrator, which widens the cycle and
  branches the repository itself. A path whose toplevel is already in the
  project's scope, the primary excepted, is not new: it gets none of your
  checks and no record, and is relayed with the rest; the orchestrator's
  own checks decide whether it rejoins the cycle — it refuses one still in
  the cycle. `drop from scope:` during a build is
  relayed and not recorded: it narrows the cycle, never the project, and
  the repository stays in the project's scope until dropped at a stop of
  yours; **HEAD check (build)** accepts what the orchestrator's drop leaves.
- *During a spike* — an experimenter stop, or **Resume**'s stop on an
  open spike. A scope phrase is refused in
  your own words: the experimenter has no stop that takes one; widen
  between cycles.

### Loop

1. **Limit check.** Digested ≥ `--count`, or the clock past `--until` →
   **End**.
2. **Plan.**

   a. **New cycle:** `C` += 1, `G` = 1.

   b. **Launch the plan.** The target is `<project dir>/plan-c<C>-g<G>.md`.
   Append `cycle <C> plan: <target>`, then `ccc run project-forester`, mode
   **plan**: + the scope, project dir, `project.md` path, latest state path or
   `none`, branch prefix, the cycle dir parent
   `<ADR root>/YYYY/MM/` and basename prefix `DD-<project slug>--` (today's
   date), every earlier plan path of this cycle, every
   `notes-plan-user-c<C>-…` path in the project dir, the target, and the
   exploration naming `<project dir>/exploration-c<C>-g<G>-<N>.md`. A
   cut-off launch → **Cut-off launch**. Route:
{# TODO(correctness-plan-out-check-recomputes-today-on-cold-resume): the parent and prefix are today's, so a plan escalation cold-resumed on a later day fails this check; see TODO.md. #}
   - `build` or `spike` → `out` must be `<cycle dir parent><basename
     prefix><cycle slug>/request.md` — the parent and basename prefix this
     launch handed, `<cycle slug>` one path segment — and the file must
     exist (`test -f`) → else route as `ESCALATE` below, surfacing `out`
     quoted in place of the lines: a cycle dir anywhere else is never
     recorded, so no **Project commit** reaches it. Then `out` is the
     cycle's `request.md`; its directory is the cycle dir.
{%- if gate %} Append `cycle
     <C> planned: <kind> <target> request <out>`. **Cycle gate:** STOP, surface the plan path, the request
     path and the kind, end turn. `go` → step 3. Any other words → written
     verbatim to `<project dir>/notes-plan-user-c<C>-g<G>.md`; `G` += 1;
     step 2b again, the same cycle.
{%- else %} Append `cycle
     <C> plan written: <kind> <target> request <out>`. Step 2c.
{%- endif %}

{% if gate %}
   - `done` → append `cycle <C> plan done: <target>`; **End**: `out` is the
     plan, which says why.
{% else %}
   - `done` → append `cycle <C> plan written: done <target> request
     none`. Step 2c.
{% endif %}
   - `ESCALATE` or a missing status line → append `cycle <C> plan
     escalated: <target> session <id>`; STOP, surface the lines it wrote
     above its status line, verbatim, plus `out` and the session id, end
     turn; a missing line surfaces `-` and the session id, its prose not
     relayed. An unrecovered launch → STOP, surface what **Cut-off launch**
     lists, end turn. The user says: `resume: <words>` → `ccc resume <id>
     "<words>"`, or with no session recoverable repeat the launch; route by
     this step — a second `ESCALATE` appends a second `plan escalated`
     line. Other words about the plan → written verbatim to
     `<project dir>/notes-plan-user-c<C>-g<G>.md`; `G` += 1; step 2b
     again. `stop` → **End**.{% if not gate %} No review runs on an
     escalation: the user is reading it.{% endif +%}
{% if not gate %}

   c. **Plan review.** The plan and the request are reviewed against the
   owner's words before the cycle runs. The plan path is the target of
   step 2b; the request path and the kind are the last `plan written`
   line's. Every launch here runs with `--cwd <primary>` and the standard
