### Project commit

At setup, before every cycle launch, at every stop and at **End**, commit
the project's records in the sidecar: the project dir, and every cycle dir
a `cycle <C> planned`{% if not gate %} or `cycle <C> plan written`{% endif %} line names (its `request` path's directory{% if not gate %}, when
not `none`{% endif %}) that no
`cycle <C> started: build` line names — rejected and replanned cycle dirs,
and spike dirs. A started build's dir is the orchestrator's, which commits
it itself. One command per directory, pathspec-limited to it, all of them
in one Bash call, `;`-separated, so a directory that fails never blocks the
others:

```
git -C <dir> add -- . && { git -C <dir> diff --cached --quiet -- . || git -C <dir> commit -m "<project slug>: project record at <stop>" -- . ; }
```

`<stop>` names the step, e.g. `cycle 3 gate`. A rejected commit → `git -C
<dir> reset -q -- .`, and the stop's lines open with `artifact commit
rejected: <dir>` and the hook's output verbatim; the next stop retries it.
It never stops the loop on its own.

### HEAD check (record)

In every repository of the scope: HEAD equals the last recorded `head` —
taken from the last line of the record that carries a value for that
repository, an `ended` line with an entry for it or its `scope added` line,
whichever comes later in the record; with neither, its `start head` — and
`git symbolic-ref --short HEAD` prints that line's `branch` for it, or its
base branch when it was the header → else STOP, name the repository, that
commit and branch, end turn: the user checks it out; nobody else moves the
repositories. Record order decides, not line kind: a repository dropped and
added again is checked against its new `scope added` line. It runs on
resume and before every cycle launch, so each cycle starts where the last
one left each stack.

### Never

Never on your own initiative. Every item here binds you, not the user; the
user's chat word lifts any of them (**User directives**), save the one
marked otherwise. **Destructive git** sets the bar for anything that
deletes or rewrites: the action and its target named.

- Touch any repository of the scope beyond reading it: `symbolic-ref`, `rev-parse`,
  `rev-list`, `merge-base`, `for-each-ref`, `check-ref-format`,
  `show-ref`, `status`. You never check out, commit, push, reset, or delete a branch,
  a leftover included: the subagents own the repositories for a cycle's duration,
  and the user owns it between cycles.
- Merge anything, anywhere, ever. No directive lifts this one: "nothing
  should be merged" is the user's own rule for the project.
- Launch a cycle while one is open.
- Answer a gate or a plan yourself, or relay anything at a gate but the
  user's words.

### End

1. **Project commit**.
2. STOP. Surface:
   - the latest state path and the project dir;
   - every line of the latest state that begins `- owed to the tree: `,
     verbatim (`grep '^- owed to the tree: ' <latest state>`; nothing when
     there is none), introduced as "tracker entries the next build was to
     file; the project ended first" — the user files them by hand, or
     starts the driver again, whose first plan is the build that files
     them;
   - the `scope:` line, when the scope holds more than the primary;
   - per repository of the scope, introduced by its path: `git -C <path>
     for-each-ref --format='%(refname:short) %(objectname:short)'
